  "updated": "2026-09-24T10:04:18.041223+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, cool, and breezy today with rough seas. \n\nThe dry and cooler than average weather we've been enjoying for the \nlast several days continues for at least one more day thanks to a \nsprawling high pressure centered over Quebec. Placement of southern \nNew England between this surface high and building low pressure\nto the north will continue the persistent and increasingly \nsteady northeast flow keeping temperatures in the low 60s (east)\nto mid/upper 60s (CT valley). Thanks to those persistent winds \nover a long fetch we'll continue to see dangerous seas with \nwaves a high as 12-15 ft over the southern outer waters. Rough \nseas will only increase in magnitude and area going into the \nweekend. Over land, we'll continue to see breezy winds gusting \nto 25-35 mph. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong coastal low pressure system will bring a \nlong duration of rain, rough seas, strong winds, and coastal \nflooding/erosion Friday through the weekend. Exact magnitude and \nplacement of the winds and rain remain uncertain. \n\nBottom line up front: prolonged rain and wind is expected this \nweekend, with the worst of it along the coast. Coastal impacts will \nalso be felt from coastal flooding and erosion, especially east\nfacing coastlines. Guidance is ever so slowly coming into \nagreement on the storm's track, leading to still only moderate \nconfidence in exact numbers/times with regard to strongest \nwinds, peak coastal flooding etc. For this reason, watches \ncontinue. However, confidence is high that this will be an \nimpactful early season nor'easter for southern New England as a \ncoastal low meandering off the eastern seaboard is captured by \nanother feature and pulled back to the west after passing near \nthe 70/40 benchmark. Latest GFS guidance seems to be caving \ntoward a more western track in line with the ECMWF/UKMET. It's \nyet to be seen if this is the start of a trend or an outlier, \nbut the 00Z GEFS continues to have quite a wide E-W spread in \nlow centers by Saturday morning. \n\nA western track would have an impact on coastal flooding, \npotentially expanding minor flooding to more southern facing \ncoastlines toward the later half of the weekend; this is hinted at \nin the Stevens Institute coastal flooding guidance and will \nneed to be monitored as we settle on an eventual low track. \nRegardless, we will no doubt be dealing with a good deal of \ncoastal erosion and at least some minor coastal flooding on the \neast coast, especially south of Boston. This will be a prolonged\ncoastal flooding event from late Fri-Sun which could exacerbate\nflooding concerns as water may not be able to fully evacuate \nbetween high tides. \n\nThe latest ensemble probabilities continue to indicate the heaviest \nrainfall over eastern MA and RI where probabilities of 2+\" have \nincreased (80%+) as have probabilities of 4\" (40-60%) centered over \neastern MA. The slow moving nature of this pattern means that this \nis spread over 48+ hours which should limit flash flooding concerns. \n\nThe strongest winds will be over the waters where Storm Watches \nremain in effect starting on Friday. On land the strongest winds \nwill be along the coast where the presence of leaves on trees will \nexacerbate potential tree damage compared to a late season storm. \nEnsemble guidance indicates >70% chance of gusts over 40 mph, while \nthe chance of gusts nearing 60 mph over Cape Cod and the islands has \ndecreased in the last 24 hours for both the EPS and GEFS likely \nowing to the further west track. The low center lingers, weakening, \nSunday and even into Monday before finally exiting east by Tuesday.",
